WEBVTT

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:05.783
It starts with an email address
No card, no sales call.

00:00:07.291 --> 00:00:14.371
A six-digit code, to prove the address works
An address nobody can reach is an account nobody can recover.

00:00:14.411 --> 00:00:19.415
What do you make?
This decides which templates come up — it is not a category you are stuck in.

00:00:19.455 --> 00:00:24.718
The name, and the address that comes with it
The web address is checked as you type.

00:00:33.924 --> 00:00:38.073
How you sell today
So the setup starts from where you already are.

00:00:38.113 --> 00:00:42.347
And what you want to end up with
Not every shop needs a checkout.

00:00:42.387 --> 00:00:45.772
Where you sell
Sets the currency and the tax rules.

00:00:45.812 --> 00:00:55.485
Templates are a direction, not a cage
Each one is a full document — palette, type, page structure — and every part of it is editable.

00:00:55.525 --> 00:01:00.355
A shop exists now
Draft, unpublished, and already a complete site.

00:01:00.395 --> 00:01:04.798
A shop needs something to sell
Four pieces, with their photographs.

00:01:04.838 --> 00:01:10.183
Name, description, price
The description is what the product page reads from.

00:01:10.223 --> 00:01:16.701
A compare-at price makes a discount
The storefront works out the percentage and strikes the old figure through.

00:01:16.741 --> 00:01:24.223
Pictures are resized in the browser
A photo straight off a phone is four megabytes; it is shrunk before it is ever sent.

00:01:31.199 --> 00:01:37.401
Four products, live
Nothing is on the internet yet — the shop has not been published.

00:01:37.679 --> 00:01:44.329
Three columns
Sections on the left, the page in the middle, the settings for whatever is selected on the right.

00:01:44.369 --> 00:01:51.963
The middle is not a preview
It renders the same components the live storefront renders. There is no second implementation to drift.

00:01:52.003 --> 00:02:00.369
The header is structural
It cannot be deleted or dragged out of place — a shop with no navigation is not a state worth being able to reach.

00:02:00.409 --> 00:02:05.031
An announcement bar
Leave it empty and the bar does not render at all.

00:02:05.071 --> 00:02:10.076
What the header carries
Search, account and basket, each optional.

00:02:10.116 --> 00:02:12.819
And whether it follows you down the page

00:02:14.666 --> 00:02:21.636
The first thing a visitor sees
Every word of it is a field on the right, and the page updates as you type.

00:02:23.037 --> 00:02:28.757
Buttons, and where they go
An internal path like /shop, or a full web address.

00:02:28.797 --> 00:02:36.279
A picture, resized on the way in
Dropped in, chosen from a file, or picked out of what this shop has already uploaded.

00:02:36.637 --> 00:02:42.924
Three arrangements
Picture beside the text, text over the picture, or no picture at all.

00:02:44.325 --> 00:02:50.101
How tall it stands
Full screen is a statement; compact gets people to the products sooner.

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:57.232
And the band it sits on
Four tones, all drawn from the shop's own palette.

00:02:59.143 --> 00:03:06.366
A new section lands below the one selected
And the canvas scrolls to meet it, so what you type next is something you can see.

00:03:06.406 --> 00:03:10.133
Two to six short points
Each is a title and a line.

00:03:11.534 --> 00:03:14.494
Across two, three or four columns

00:03:15.894 --> 00:03:23.291
A picture beside a paragraph
The workhorse of an about page, and of any shop that sells on how a thing is made.

00:03:24.691 --> 00:03:30.296
The picture can swap sides
Alternating them down a page is what stops it reading as a list.

00:03:31.518 --> 00:03:38.881
The catalogue, on the page
These are the four real products added earlier — their photographs, their prices, their discount.

00:03:38.921 --> 00:03:44.133
Which products, and how many
Newest first, featured only, or everything.

00:03:44.173 --> 00:03:49.607
The cards themselves
Columns, picture shape, and whether the card has an edge.

00:03:51.007 --> 00:03:57.039
Prices can come off entirely
Which is what a catalogue that takes enquiries rather than orders wants.

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:02.936
Your story, in your own words
Blank lines make paragraphs.

00:04:06.427 --> 00:04:13.141
Narrow, because lines that long are hard to read
Sixty characters, which is where the eye stops tracking.

00:04:14.179 --> 00:04:21.604
A grid of photographs
Pictures are uploaded here, reordered with the arrows, and each one gets a line of alt text.

00:04:21.644 --> 00:04:27.078
Several at once
Dropped in, or chosen from a file — and resized before they are sent.

00:04:28.479 --> 00:04:34.519
Alt text, beside the picture
A gallery with none is a wall of nothing to a screen reader.

00:04:34.559 --> 00:04:38.542
Order is the feature
The arrows move a picture forward or back.

00:04:39.943 --> 00:04:44.439
What customers have said
A quote, a name, and where they are from.

00:04:46.390 --> 00:04:49.606
Several side by side, or one held up large

00:04:51.006 --> 00:04:57.635
The questions you keep getting
Answering them on the page is cheaper than answering them one at a time in a chat window.

00:05:00.305 --> 00:05:07.190
Collecting email addresses
The form is real: addresses land in the dashboard, not in a third-party widget.

00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:15.731
WhatsApp, Instagram and email
Three buttons that open the apps people already use, rather than a form nobody trusts.

00:05:17.131 --> 00:05:24.250
Each can be switched off
The numbers themselves live in Store settings, once, rather than on every section.

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:32.199
And the small print
Structural, like the header — links, socials and the policies a shop is required to publish.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:42.234
Click away from everything, and the panel becomes the theme
One palette, one type pairing, one set of shapes — applied to every page at once.

00:05:42.274 --> 00:05:51.105
Colour, as nine roles
Not a free-for-all: “Buttons” and “Highlights” mean something, so changing one stays coherent everywhere it lands.

00:05:51.145 --> 00:05:57.980
Type
Nine faces, self-hosted — a shopper's browser never has to tell a font CDN who they are.

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:03.710
And shape
Corner rounding, button rounding, and how much air sits between sections.

00:06:05.110 --> 00:06:11.696
The whole page, as a list
Reorder it, hide a section without deleting it, or jump straight to one.

00:06:11.736 --> 00:06:20.548
Phone and tablet are widths, not emulations
The storefront is responsive, so narrowing the canvas shows exactly what a phone shows.

00:06:20.588 --> 00:06:25.550
And every change is undoable
Typing a heading is one step, not forty.

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:34.936
None of this is public yet
Preview saves the draft first, then opens it in a tab — so what you look at is what is stored, not what happened to be on screen.

00:06:34.976 --> 00:06:44.762
The draft, as a customer would get it
No outlines, no panels, links between pages working. The band across the top is the only thing that is not the shop.

00:06:46.163 --> 00:06:55.394
Publishing checks the shop is fit to open
A shop with no delivery option has a checkout that cannot complete; it says so rather than letting it go live.

00:06:55.434 --> 00:07:02.660
Live
Published versions are immutable snapshots — rolling back is choosing an earlier one, not undoing anything.

00:07:02.711 --> 00:07:08.231
And this is what a customer gets
The same components, the same document, none of the editor.

00:07:09.631 --> 00:07:16.599
Product pages are generated, not built
They come from the catalogue, so a new product needs no layout work at all.

00:07:16.639 --> 00:07:25.695
Adding something to a basket
The button says what happened, then goes back to being a button. No modal, no interstitial, nothing to dismiss.

00:07:25.735 --> 00:07:31.736
The basket itself
Quantities, line totals, and a discount code if the shop is running one.

00:07:31.776 --> 00:07:43.183
Totals come back from the server
Never worked out in the browser. A basket showing a figure the server would not agree with only reveals itself at the payment step, which is the worst place to find out.

00:07:44.553 --> 00:07:57.496
One page, and no account unless the shop wants one
Guest checkout is the default, and nobody ever abandoned a basket because they were not asked to invent a password first. Whether to insist is the merchant’s to decide.

00:07:58.897 --> 00:08:05.953
Where it is going
Only the fields the order actually needs — a digital product would not ask for any of this.

00:08:05.999 --> 00:08:13.737
Delivery, priced by the shop
These come from the rates the merchant set up; the total re-adds itself as the choice changes.

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:25.223
This card is going to be refused
Four thousand, then zeroes, then two: the number every provider reserves for a decline. Worth watching what the shop does with it.

00:08:27.107 --> 00:08:39.539
A refused card leaves a payable order, not an empty basket
The order exists and the basket is closed. The customer pays against the order rather than building it a second time and hoping the stock held.

00:08:39.669 --> 00:08:44.591
So the card is the only thing to try again
Everything already typed stays typed.

00:08:46.944 --> 00:08:57.925
Paid
Payments are simulated in this build — the order, its states and the refund that follows are all real, but no money has moved. A live provider replaces this one step.

00:08:58.063 --> 00:09:03.668
And a moment later, on the shop's side
The same order, in the merchant's dashboard.

00:09:05.068 --> 00:09:15.964
An order is a state machine, not a status field
It can only move to a state that follows the one it is in, so “delivered” is not even offered until it has been sent.

00:09:17.364 --> 00:09:25.956
Refunds are partial by default
A chipped piece is worth a thousand rupees back, not the whole order and a return postage argument.

00:09:27.535 --> 00:09:37.577
And the figure on the dashboard is net
Paid, less what went back. A revenue number that counts refunded money is a number that lies to the person relying on it.

00:09:38.977 --> 00:09:49.104
Who can have an account, and how
Three separate decisions, because a bakery taking orders over WhatsApp and a seller of digital downloads do not want the same answer.

00:09:50.505 --> 00:10:02.312
An email address, a mobile number, or either
Signing in by mobile sends a text message for every code, so it belongs to a paid plan — and the option says which one rather than sitting there dead.

00:10:02.352 --> 00:10:15.455
A password, a code, and when to insist
Confirming before the first order rather than at sign-up: somebody browsing can fill a basket straight away, and the code is asked for when a wrong address would actually cost them something.

00:10:15.495 --> 00:10:25.196
The person who already ordered as a guest
Harshini checked out without an account. The shop is already holding her address, her phone number and what she bought.

00:10:27.605 --> 00:10:39.268
So it asks for a code, not a password
A record that has already ordered here is never handed over on the strength of typing the address. Otherwise anybody could read somebody else's orders.

00:10:40.669 --> 00:10:51.309
And the order she placed as a guest is hers
The same record, claimed rather than duplicated — and the order itself was never moved. Proving the address is what earns the right to see it.
